'''Lake Hāwea''' is New Zealand's [[List of lakes in New Zealand#Largest lakes|ninth largest lake]] located in the [[South Island]]'s [[Otago]] Region at an altitude of 348 m. It covers approximately 151 km<sup>2</sup> and is 392 m deep.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

Lake Hāwea is named after a [[Māori people|Māori]] tribe who preceded the [[Waitaha (South Island iwi)|Waitaha]] people in the area.<ref name="DoC"/>

Lake Hāwea stretches 35 km from north to south. It lies in a [[U-shaped valley|glacial valley]] formed during the last [[ice age]], and is fed by the [[Hunter River (New Zealand)|Hunter River]]. Nearby [[Lake Wānaka]] lies in a parallel glacial valley 8 km to the west. At their closest point, a rocky ridge called The Neck, the lakes are only 1 km apart.
[[File:Hawea 27.jpg|thumb|Lake Hāwea in summer]]
Lake Hāwea is dammed to the south by an ancient [[terminal moraine]] created 10,000 years ago. In 1958, the lake was artificially raised 20 metres to store more water for increased [[hydroelectric]] power generation at the [[Roxburgh Dam]].<ref name=TeAraOtagoPlaces>{{cite encyclopedia
|encyclopedia=Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand
|author=Malcolm McKinnon
|title=Otago places – Clutha River/Mata-Au
|date=2009
|url=http://www.TeAra.govt.nz/en/otago-places/page-12
|accessdate=13 July 2017
}}</ref>

The only flat land around the lake is at its southern end, surrounding its outflow into the [[Hāwea River]], a short tributary of the [[Clutha River|Clutha 
/ Matau-au]], which it joins near [[Albert Town, New Zealand|Albert Town]]. The settlement of [[Lake Hāwea (town)|Lake Hāwea]] is found at the lake's southern shore.

The lake is a popular resort, well used in the summer for fishing, boating and swimming. The nearby mountains and fast-flowing rivers allow for [[Adventure travel|adventure tourism]] year-round, such as [[jetboat]]ing and [[skiing]].

==Māori history==

For [[Māori people|Māori]], the Wānaka and Hāwea area was a natural crossroads. The [[Haast Pass]] led to the [[West Coast, New Zealand|West Coast]] and its [[pounamu]] (greenstone); the [[Cardrona Valley]] led to the natural rock bridge "Whatatorere" which was the only place that the [[Kawarau River]] and [[Clutha River|Clutha River / Mata-Au]] could be crossed without boats.<ref name="DoC">{{cite book
|title=Bannockburn Heritage Landscape Study
|url=http://www.doc.govt.nz/Documents/science-and-technical/SfC244b.pdf
|date=2004
|first1=Janet |last1=Stephenson |first2=Heather |last2=Bauchop |first3=Peter |last3=Petchey
|page=29
}}</ref> {{Lang|mi|[[Mōkihi]]}} reed boats enabled a swift return downriver to the east coast.

The Cromwell basin supported a large population of [[moa]], which were hunted to extinction about 500 years ago.<ref name="DoC"/>

Until the early nineteenth century, the area was visited annually by [[Ngāi Tahu]] who sought pounamu in the mountains above the [[Haast River]] and hunted eels and birds over summer, returning to the east coast by descending the [[Clutha River|Clutha River / Mata-Au]] in reed boats.<ref name=TeAraOtagoMaori>
{{cite encyclopedia
| url=https://teara.govt.nz/en/otago-region/page-4
| author=Malcolm McKinnon
| title=Otago region – Māori history and whaling
| date=2009
| encyclopedia=Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand
| accessdate=2017-07-11}}</ref>  Ngāi Tahu use of the land was ended by [[History of the Otago Region#Ngati Toa invasions|attacks by North Island tribes]].<ref name="DoC"/> In 1836, the [[Ngāti Tama]] chief [[Te Pūoho]] led a 100-person war party, armed with muskets, down the West Coast and over the Haast Pass: they fell on the Ngāi Tahu encampment between [[Lake Wānaka]] and Lake Hāwea, capturing 10 people and killing and eating two children.<ref name=Smith1910>{{cite book
|title=History and Traditions of the Maoris of the West Coast North Island of New Zealand Prior to 1840
|author=S Percy Smith
|date=1910
|publisher = Polynesian Society
|location=New Plymouth
|url=http://nzetc.victoria.ac.nz/tm/scholarly/tei-SmiHist-t1-body1-d21-d7.html
}}</ref> Although Te Pūoho was later killed by the southern Ngāi Tahu leader [[Tūhawaiki]],<ref name=AthollAnderson>{{cite encyclopedia
|author=Atholl Anderson
|title =Te Puoho-o-te-rangi
|volume =1
|year=1990
|encyclopedia=Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand
|url=http://www.TeAra.govt.nz/en/biographies/1t59/te-puoho-o-te-rangi
|accessdate =2017-07-11}}</ref> Maori seasonal visits to the area ceased.

The first European to see the lake was [[Nathanael Chalmers]] in 1853.<ref>{{cite encyclopedia |author=Jock Phillips
|title=European exploration – Otago and Southland
|date=2006
|encyclopedia=Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand
|url=http://www.TeAra.govt.nz/en/european-exploration/page-7
|accessdate=2017-07-11
}}
</ref> Guided by Reko and Kaikōura, he walked from [[Tuturau]] ([[Southland, New Zealand|Southland]]) to the lakes via the [[Kawarau River]]. He was stricken by dysentery, so his guides returned him down the Clutha in a reed boat.<ref name="TeAraNC">
{{cite encyclopedia
|author=Roger Frazer
|title=Chalmers, Nathanael
|series=Dictionary of New Zealand Biography
|volume=1
|date=1990
|encyclopedia=Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand
|url=http://www.TeAra.govt.nz/en/biographies/1c11/chalmers-nathanael
